## Proof

By Euler's criterion, for $1 \le k \le p-1$,
$$a_k = k^{(p-1)/2} \equiv \left(\frac{k}{p}\right) \pmod{p},$$
the Legendre symbol. Hence in $\mathbb{F}_p[x]$,
$$q(x) = \sum_{k=1}^{p-1} \left(\frac{k}{p}\right) x^k.$$

The desired $n$ is the multiplicity of $x=1$ as a root of $q$. Substitute $x = 1+t$ and expand:
$$q(1+t) = \sum_{k=1}^{p-1} \left(\frac{k}{p}\right)(1+t)^k = \sum_{j \ge 0} S_j\, t^j, \qquad S_j := \sum_{k=1}^{p-1} \left(\frac{k}{p}\right)\binom{k}{j}.$$
Then $n$ is the smallest $j$ with $S_j \not\equiv 0 \pmod{p}$.

**Power sums.** Define $T_m := \sum_{k=1}^{p-1} \left(\frac{k}{p}\right) k^m \pmod{p}$. Using $\left(\frac{k}{p}\right) \equiv k^{(p-1)/2}$,
$$T_m \equiv \sum_{k=1}^{p-1} k^{\,m+(p-1)/2} \pmod{p}.$$
The classical identity $\sum_{k=1}^{p-1} k^r \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ if $(p-1)\nmid r$ (for $r\ge 1$), and $\equiv -1 \pmod{p}$ if $(p-1)\mid r$, yields
$$T_m \equiv \begin{cases} -1 & \text{if } m \equiv \tfrac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p-1},\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases} \pmod{p}$$

**Relating $S_j$ to $T_m$.** Since $\binom{k}{j} = \frac{1}{j!}\,k^{\underline{j}}$ and $k^{\underline{j}} = k^j + (\text{polynomial in }k\text{ of degree }< j)$,
$$S_j = \frac{1}{j!}\,T_j + \sum_{i<j} c_i\, T_i \qquad (c_i \in \mathbb{F}_p).$$

**For $j < \tfrac{p-1}{2}$:** Every $T_i$ with $0 \le i \le j < \tfrac{p-1}{2}$ satisfies $i \not\equiv \tfrac{p-1}{2}\pmod{p-1}$ (as $0 \le i < \tfrac{p-1}{2} < p-1$), so $T_i = 0$. Hence $S_j = 0$.

**For $j = \tfrac{p-1}{2}$:** All $T_i = 0$ for $i < j$, and $T_j = -1$. Thus
$$S_{(p-1)/2} = \frac{-1}{\bigl(\tfrac{p-1}{2}\bigr)!} \pmod{p}.$$
Since $\bigl(\tfrac{p-1}{2}\bigr)!$ is not divisible by $p$ (its argument is $< p$), this is nonzero in $\mathbb{F}_p$.

Therefore $S_j = 0$ for all $j < \tfrac{p-1}{2}$ and $S_{(p-1)/2} \ne 0$, so the order of vanishing of $q$ at $x=1$ is exactly $\tfrac{p-1}{2}$.

The greatest nonnegative integer $n$ such that $(x-1)^n \mid q(x)$ in $\mathbb{F}_p[x]$ is

$$\boxed{n = \dfrac{p-1}{2}}.$$

